--[ CHAPTER I : GENETIC ALGORITHMS (NAVIGATING DISCRETE HYPER-SPACES)
===========================================================================

### 1.1 What is a Genetic Algorithm (GA)?

A Genetic Algorithm is a stochastic optimization meta-heuristic inspired by 
the principles of Darwinian natural selection. Instead of iterating 
through solutions sequentially, a GA evaluates a "population" of random 
candidate solutions, scoring them via a "fitness function". The best 
performers are selected to exchange data (Crossover) and undergo random 
alterations (Mutation) to produce a new, theoretically superior generation.

### 1.2 The Scientific Imperative: Escaping Local Optima
Why apply this to the Steckerbrett? The plugboard configuration is a classic 
combinatorial optimization problem. The topology of this search space is highly 
irregular; it is filled with "local optima" -- configurations that decrypt a few 
words correctly but are ultimately wrong.

If we used a standard hill-climbing algorithm, the system would find a partial 
decryption, climb to the top of that specific mathematical "hill", and stop, 
believing it had solved the cipher. 

The GA solves this through **Mutation**. 

Let S be the search space of all possible plugboard configurations. Let f(x) 
be the fitness function for a configuration x in S. The GA does not rely on 
the gradient DELTA f(x). Instead, when the algorithm gets trapped in a local 
optimum, the mutation rate (e.g., a 25% chance to blindly swap two wires) acts 
as a probabilistic "teleportation" mechanism. It forces the algorithm to jump 
across the hyper-space, ensuring global exploration while the crossover 
mechanism ensures local exploitation.

