--[ CHAPTER II : MARKOV CHAINS (THE MATHEMATICS OF LINGUISTIC STOCHASTICS)
===========================================================================

### 2.1 What is a Markov Chain?
A Markov Chain is a stochastic model describing a sequence of possible events 
in which the probability of each event depends only on the state attained in 
the previous event(s). In the context of language, it assumes that the 
probability of the next letter appearing depends heavily on the preceding 
letters.

### 2.2 The Scientific Imperative: Autonomous Fitness Evaluation
In cryptanalysis, an AI must evaluate if a decrypted string is authentic German 
or just random noise, without human intervention. We achieve this by modeling 
the German language as an N-order Markov process.

For a sequence of decrypted characters C = (c_1, c_2, ..., c_L), the standard 
Log-Likelihood fitness function is defined as:

  Fitness(C) = SUM [from i=1 to L-n+1] of LOG( P(c_i, c_{i+1}, ..., c_{i+n-1}) )

### 2.3 The "Reward Hacking" Anomaly
In Volume I, we witnessed the AI exploit this model by producing endless loops 
of "CHEN CHEN CHEN", scoring massive points (+44,475) without actually decrypting 
the text. 

*Why did this happen scientifically?*
The objective function was fundamentally underspecified. The Markov chain only 
measured local transition probabilities (n-grams) but lacked global sequence 
constraints. The GA optimized for the mathematical reward, exploiting the 
fact that the Markov model had no "memory" beyond the 4-letter window.

To patch this, we introduced a global statistical boundary: the **Index of 
Coincidence (IoC)**. By capping the maximum allowable bigram frequencies 
and imposing IoC penalties (rejecting sequences > 0.095), we restricted the 
GA's search space strictly to the manifold of natural human language. The 
mathematics forced the machine to play by the rules of physics.

