### 3.1 What is the Viterbi Algorithm (HMM)?
Natural Language Processing (NLP) bridges the gap between raw data and semantic 
meaning. Central to NLP is the Hidden Markov Model (HMM). The Viterbi algorithm 
is a dynamic programming algorithm used to find the most likely sequence of 
hidden states (the actual, space-separated words) that results in a sequence 
of observed events (the continuous, unspaced ciphertext).

### 3.2 The Scientific Imperative: Algorithmic Text Segmentation
Military Enigma operators transmitted data in unbroken 5-letter blocks (e.g., 
"DASBO OTIST") to obfuscate word length boundaries. 

If a decrypted string has length N, there are 2^(N-1) possible ways to 
insert spaces. For a 100-letter message, testing all combinations yields 
6.3 x 10^29 permutations. 

The Viterbi algorithm reduces this exponential nightmare to linear time complexity 
O(N * |V|^2), where |V| is the vocabulary size. It does this by building 
a trellis graph and calculating the Maximum A Posteriori (MAP) probability.

Let y_{1:T} be the observed continuous characters, and x_{1:T} be the hidden 
states (valid dictionary words). The algorithm computes the optimal path 
probability V[t,k] at step t for state k:

  V[t,k] = MAX_over_x ( V[t-1, x] * P(y_t | k) * P(k | x) )

*   Emission Probability P(y_t | k): Is this string slice a valid word in 
    the Wehrmacht dictionary?
*   Transition Probability P(k | x): What is the grammatical likelihood of 
    word k following word x?

*Example in execution:*
When the algorithm analyzes the block "VER SEN KT", it evaluates the branches. 
The node path [VER] -> [SENKT] survives because both are mathematically probable 
in the transition matrix. The path [V] -> [ERSEN] -> [KT] is annihilated 
(pruned) because the emission probability of [KT] as a standalone word 
approaches zero.

